        <Title>Beginning</Title>
        <Text>Every artist who has ever tried to capture the beauty of nature has failed. The problem was always the same: art is seduction. It’s purpose is to divert attention away from what is really there  beauty was out there in the world and connected to everything else, so to truly capture it, one would have to cut it out and that would kill it. Not only was that impossible to do, it was disrespectful.
Mandri burped.
He took a deep swig of his burgeon hooch and finished it off. He licked his lips, then rummaged around in his handcart, looking the other skin of hooch that he knew was hiding under the mass of rolled up paintings. It was his last skin of burgeon hooch. It was a simple hooch, just a bit of bit of water, sugar, and the burgeons he’d bought from Geem. All Geem had was down-valley burgeons, decent stuff, but the waxy capsule leaves split too easy, meaning the insides would dry out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to, but that was all Geem had, so he bought them. This burgeon hooch was far from tasty, but it was enough to make his long walk through the country tolerable.
He found the hooch and took a swig. He took a longer swig, then continued spiraling over the fact that he’d ever picked up a paintbrush.
Because what was a painting, really? Paint a tree, and all those leaves get reduced to flecks of green. A living leaf with all its complexity, its pattern, its anastomosing veins pumped full of vital fluids, its green birth and brown death, its fall from the branch, its decay into dirt, that whole nested universe, reduced to a fleck of green. And this gets to the truth that no artist wants to admit: art has nothing to do with the truth. Art is the basest form of seduction. It’s about ‘look at this, and please ignore that’. Paint an oasis, and look, there’s a naked woman bathing in the mossy water. Focus the viewer’s gaze by sharpening the droplets of jade that gleam on her cinnamon skin. The leaf? Feather its edge into the sky. Blame it on the effects of atmospheric haze. Better yet, don’t paint the leaf, paint bunches of leafy shapes. Think about removing the tree entirely, because it’s good practice to erase all evidence of a crime after it’s been committed.
Seduction drenches every fiber of the artist’s being. They’re seduced by themselves. They see the wonder of creation and think, “People ought to know about my interpretation,” as if Solraheen’s creation was not already speaking. And this is true: every painting, every poem, they’re all cutting god off mid-sentence. And somehow people encourage it. Crowds of enablers herd themselves into art galleries to admire the pretty displays self-absorption. The complexity of reality is right outside, but they’d all rather see reality cornered, killed, flayed, then put on display, with choice sections of its hide stitched into pretty frames. The world is easier to digest that way. Makes one feel less like they understand what its all about.
“Go and paint all this,” Mandri said, sweeping his arm over the sunset. “It’s not possible. Take as long as you like. Take a lifetime. Take ten lifetimes! Then… and then…”
And then he forgot what he was on about because a lone farmer in the field to his left was staring at him.
A potential client.

Mandri ambles of the country lane, turning his handcart with some difficulty because he had to lift the damn thing up to turn it. Broken swing gate.
“Don’t bother!” the old man called. “Whatever ya sellin’, I ain’t buyin’!”
Mandri, still struggling with his cart, pretended not to hear. “Hello!” he called back. And with his cart straightened, he proceeded down the farmers lane with a big, salesman’s smile, while the skinny wheels of his cart squeaked in their grease. 
Licensed vagrancy required a good amount of tact and guile, as well as willingness to deceive. He lacked a natural these virtues. And to be painting peddler, which was his official trade, . His route through life had given him , and the only way to make money  He did it because he had, long ago
Desperate to be ambushed by some incredible work that . The unfortunate reality is that the vast, vast majority of paintings produced in the past few hundred years were , and over the past year he’d come to the only things worth seeing were the things that burgeon. 

They were down-valley burgeons, decent stuff, but the waxy capsule leaves were liable to split. The insides would dry out before he could get to them in the ways that he wanted to. But, that was all Geem had, so he bought them.


 It was an ox. It was the same damned yellow-headed ox from Vigram’s leech farm.
What were the chances?
This far out?
“Mruuuuu,” the ox said when it noticed him gawking. It sauntered through burgeon addled haze, dewlap flapping. The skip in its step suggested it recognized him. And as it came into focus, the state of its distress became all too clear: red scrapes all over, courtesy of the thorns surrounding Vigram’s farm. Purple bruises splotched its armpits. The rest of its shaven body was a grotesque mosaic of little blood rings where leeches used to be. Those leeches still attached were concentrated on its haunches, with a few suckling on its belly.
The poor thing was obviously unable to care for itself, and so it was coming to him for help. It had no idea where it was. Absent a miracle, it was going to die.
Mandri’s heart went out to the creature.
“I can’t do anything for you,” he said, but even as he said it, he knew it wasn’t true. He could do something. He could scrape off those leeches. He could lead the beast to water. He could go to the extreme and devote the rest of his meaningless life to caring for that ox. He wasn’t going to do that, but he could! He could. Should he? The answer was an obvious no, because this was only an ox. Thousands of oxes just like it were being butchered and eaten throughout the world. He’d eaten plenty of beef himself. Helping this ox and not the others would make him a hypocrite. There was a whole leech farm full of animals that needed saving down the road, and he hadn’t spared one thought for helping any of them. Were they somehow less worthy than this ox? Was he somehow responsible for the life of every living being in all creation? Where was the line?
Technically, he did own this ox. That purchase involved a tacit of assumption of responsibility. But, he hadn’t actually expected to ever see the beast again. Why was it here? How? It had wandered off Vigram’s farm, and just so happened to find his own?
He suspected a setup of some sort. Fate was never so neat. Or, maybe the ox itself was intelligent. Some say newborn gods can take the form of animals.
He cast a skeptical eye upon the ox as it picked its path around the crooked bean poles. It was clearly too dumb to be a god. And too weak. Poor thing, the whites of its eyes were red from being forced to sniff all that freeweed smoke.
It came to a stop just feet from him. The fluid inside the fat sun blisters on its back continued to swish around.
“Damn,” Mandri muttered.
The beast really needed help.
But, so did he. That was the way of the world. Creatures had to take care themselves. There just wasn’t enough concern to go around.
Mandri sipped his hooch. He held his hand up to bless the ox. 
“I release you from my service,” he said in regal tones.
He took another sip. The burgeonsight had the short yellow hairs on the ox’s head swirling. He sipped his hooch again and downed the dregs. He had more hooch sitting inside his hornscrub tree and wanted it.
“Stop looking at me like that,” he said. “I can’t do anything for you.” 
Now that he’d said what he said, it could not be taken back.
He made for his tree.
The damned ox followed him.
He sat down in the crook of his tree. The ox sat down nearby—it lay on its belly like it hadn’t done in over a week. It’s heavy ribs squished a few leeches.
“Do we know each other?” Mandri asked.
The ox stared back at him, full of bovine dumbness. Its big, dozing breaths rolled its bleeding hide back and forth over its sagging spine. Now that Mandri was positively high on burgeons, the sight of that rolling hide was a sight to see. The skin appeared to boil.
He nestled deeper into the tree trunk’s embrace as the nightly breeze caressed his cheek. He sighed a little half-sipped, absent sigh, the kind of sigh that he’d make whenever he was ready for a restful sleep. He let his eyelids slouch as he watched flies buzz in and out of ox’s snout. The ox snorted, blowing those fuzzy black dots out of his great big nostrils. The flies zipped right back in to lick at the only reliable source of moisture that they could find in this godforsaken field.

He continued along the road, stomping his way through the muddy wheel-ruts, slapping away the flies, humming nursery rhymes, trying anything to distract himself from the thought that he only had one last , and no real prospects for a client to provide him with more.

He didn’t put much stock in philosophies dreamed up while high on burgeons, but in private, eyes closed, he indulged. And who knows? He could see the pattern everywhere: Inhale, Exhale; Hot, Cold; Come, Go. Perhaps the being at the bottom of everything was a pair of lungs, and all of existence shared the same extensive breath, a cosmic exhale motivating all life.
He began to dream about a cosmic inhale, where everything moved, and talked, and breathed in reverse, all the way back to the beginning of time.
